## 1.  What are **attributes**?

Attributes in C# are *metadata* that you attach to code elements (types, members, parameters, assemblies, etc.).  
They are **classes** that derive from `System.Attribute`.  
When you compile the code, the compiler stores the attribute information in the assembly’s metadata, and the CLR keeps it around so you can read it later (usually at runtime).

Typical built‑in attributes:

| Attribute | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `[Serializable]` | Marks a type that can be serialized. |
| `[Obsolete]` | Flags that a member should not be used. |
| `[DllImport]` | Declares a P/Invoke call. |
| `[AttributeUsage]` | Controls where an attribute can be applied. |

### Defining your own attribute

```csharp
using System;

/// <summary>
///  Controls where this attribute can be applied.
///  * Targets: class or method.
///  * Inherited: false – the attribute does not flow to derived types.
///  * AllowMultiple: true – you can apply it more than once.
/// </summary>
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Method,
                Inherited = false,
                AllowMultiple = true)]
public sealed class MyInfoAttribute : Attribute
{
    public string Description { get; }

    public MyInfoAttribute(string description)
    {
        Description = description;
    }
}
```

### Applying the attribute

```csharp
[MyInfo("This is a sample class")]
public class SampleClass
{
    [MyInfo("This method does something")]
    public void DoWork()
    {
        // …
    }
}
```

> **Note**  
> The attribute class is named `MyInfoAttribute`, but when you apply it you drop the `Attribute` suffix – `[MyInfo(...)]`.

---

## 2.  What is **reflection**?

Reflection is the ability of a program to examine its own structure at runtime.  
With `System.Reflection` you can:

* Discover what types exist in an assembly.
* Inspect constructors, properties, fields, methods, events, etc.
* Read the attributes that have been applied to those members.
* Create objects, invoke methods, and set properties *dynamically*.

Reflection is the runtime counterpart of the compile‑time metadata that attributes add.

---

## 3.  A simple “read attributes” example

Below is a complete, runnable console app that:

1. Defines a custom attribute.
2. Applies it to a class and a method.
3. Uses reflection to read and print the attribute data.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Reflection;

// 1. Define the custom attribute
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Method,
                Inherited = false,
                AllowMultiple = true)]
public sealed class MyInfoAttribute : Attribute
{
    public string Description { get; }

    public MyInfoAttribute(string description)
    {
        Description = description;
    }
}

// 2. Apply the attribute
[MyInfo("This is a sample class")]
public class SampleClass
{
    [MyInfo("This method does something")]
    public void DoWork()
    {
        // Implementation omitted
    }
}

// 3. Read the attributes at runtime
public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Get the type object for SampleClass
        Type type = typeof(SampleClass);

        // ---- Read class‑level attributes ----
        Console.WriteLine("Class attributes:");
        foreach (var attr in type.GetCustomAttributes(typeof(MyInfoAttribute), false))
        {
            var myAttr = (MyInfoAttribute)attr;
            Console.WriteLine($"  {myAttr.Description}");
        }

        // ---- Read method‑level attributes ----
        Console.WriteLine("\nMethod attributes:");
        MethodInfo method = type.GetMethod("DoWork");
        foreach (var attr in method.GetCustomAttributes(typeof(MyInfoAttribute), false))
        {
            var myAttr = (MyInfoAttribute)attr;
            Console.WriteLine($"  {myAttr.Description}");
        }
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see when you run it

```
Class attributes:
  This is a sample class

Method attributes:
  This method does something
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why |
|------|--------------|-----|
| `typeof(SampleClass)` | Gets a `Type` object representing the class. | `Type` is the central reflection class. |
| `GetCustomAttributes(...)` | Returns an array of attribute instances applied to the target. | The CLR has stored the attribute data during compilation. |
| Casting to `MyInfoAttribute` | Gives you typed access to the attribute’s properties. | Allows you to read the information you put in. |

